<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Allectus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 296</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Allectus was a Roman usurper who ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> from 293 to 296. A finance official under the breakaway emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carausius">Carausius</a>, he murdered him in 293, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantius-i">Constantius Chlorus</a> had taken <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boulogne-sur-mer">Boulogne</a>, and took power. In 296 Constantius invaded, and his prefect Asclepiodotus defeated and killed Allectus, returning Britain to the empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/allectus.jpg" alt="Allectus" width="320" height="164" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Quinarius_Allectus_galley-RIC_0128.2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Allectus served as treasurer to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carausius">Carausius</a>, a Menapian officer in the Roman navy who had seized power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> in 286. In 293 Carausius became isolated when the western Caesar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantius-i">Constantius Chlorus</a>, retook some of his Gallic territories, above all the crucial port of Bononia, modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boulogne-sur-mer">Boulogne</a>, and defeated his Frankish allies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavia">Batavia</a>. Allectus assassinated Carausius and took command himself.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Allectus's reign has left little record. His coin issues are distributed much like those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carausius">Carausius</a>, and they are found in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, which indicates that the recapture of Bononia did not end the rebel empire on that side of the Channel. Carausius had used his coinage as propaganda, and some of his slogans, such as a claim to have restored liberty, were designed to appeal to British sentiment.</p><p>In September 296 Constantius launched an invasion by sea in several divisions. He led one from Bononia but seems to have been delayed by bad weather. Another division, under the praetorian prefect Asclepiodotus, used fog to avoid Allectus's ships stationed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isle-of-wight">Isle of Wight</a> and landed near Southampton Water, where the troops burnt their ships. Allectus's forces retreated from the coast but were cut off by another of Constantius's divisions and defeated. Allectus was killed in the battle, having removed all his insignia in the hope that his body would not be identified. Archaeology suggests that his defeat took place at or near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calleva-atrebatum">Calleva Atrebatum</a>, modern Silchester.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>A group of Roman troops separated from the main body by the fog caught up with the remnants of Allectus's men, mostly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/londinium">Londinium</a> and massacred them. Constantius seems not to have reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> until it was all over, and his panegyrist claims that the Britons welcomed him as a liberator. A medal struck after the victory calls him redditor lucis aeternae, restorer of the eternal light.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geoffrey-of-monmouth">Geoffrey of Monmouth</a> included Allectus in his History of the Kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, written around 1136, as an officer sent with three legions to depose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carausius">Carausius</a>, who is there a native British king. In that account his rule proves oppressive, and Asclepiodotus, here the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a>, deposes him. A coin showing his head, found in Dover in March 2019 by a metal detectorist, sold for £552,000 at auction. Rosemary Sutcliff's 1957 novel The Silver Branch is built around these events.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Allectus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 293 Constantius Chlorus captured Boulogne, and Carausius was murdered by his finance minister <b>Allectus</b>, who took power.</blockquote><a href="/state/carausian-empire">Carausian Empire</a></li></ul></section>
